        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Corded Ware culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Most of continental northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and the Baltic to northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-russia">European Russia</a>, with coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>; sites include Bronocice, Eulau, Esperstedt, Zeewijk and Turinge</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 2900–2300 BC, from the Late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> through the Copper Age to the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, dated by dendrochronology, only from 2750 to 2400 BC; regional groups include the Middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>, Fatyanovo–Balanovo, Single Grave, Scandinavian Battle Axe (from c. 2800 BC) and Finnish Battle Axe cultures</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">pottery decorated with twisted cord; single burial under small mounds in a crouched position; stone battle axes as men's grave goods; wheeled vehicles; steppe ancestry shared with the Yamnaya; a likely role in spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European languages</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Named by Friedrich Klopfleisch in 1883; the Single Grave culture defined by Andreas Peter Madsen; mostly known from burials; ancient DNA studies including Haak et al. (2015), Allentoft et al. (2015), Saag et al. (2017) and Papac et al. (2021)</span></div>
